Built stdlib-only `compress.py` that round-trips `corpus.txt` via `python3 compress.py corpus.txt out.bin` and `python3 compress.py --decompress out.bin restored.txt`. The program tries multiple stdlib compressors/settings (zlib, bz2, lzma) and stores the best result with a small header for decoding. Verified correctness with a byte-identical restore, and registered an assay that returns 0 on any mismatch or otherwise prints percentage size reduction. Current score is stable at 93.3771% reduction, which beats the trivial zlib default baseline of 88.7254%. I also ran file-level variants including broader LZMA/raw options; none beat the incumbent, so the best tested version remains in place.